A||||26||Ozzy||ECM Diags. & Blower info. Suggestions please.
Z||000000||Ozzy||08-06-99||11:47 PM||||Hey folks...Need some assistance if anyone can help. <p>I have just recently bought a '88 Coupe, 2.5L, 5 speed, Rebuild bored over, headers, cold port injection. Does 138hp on the dyno I'm told. Anyway, it basically runs well but for some maintenace type problems from what I can tell. I have a shop manual and have been doing some of the trouble shooting suggested but one problem still exists. I keep getting a code 44. <p>Now I had noticed the power in the upper range was weak intermittently. Basically a slight hesitation passed 2500 rpm. I have swapped out the coolant sensor as the old one was shot. I found a small vacuum leak in the fuel recovery lines and replaced the bad hoses. I investigated as best I could around the base of the throttle body for leaks as the manual suggests and can see nothing obvious. Nor can I see any obvious problems around the intake manifold but it is hard to say. I'm told by the guy who owned it, a mechanic by trade, that he has replaced the O2 sensor not that long ago. Also I have replaced the throttle position sensor because I was told the readings were indicating that it was bad. I figured for $20 what the hell. None of these items change the upper rpm power loss. Today I changed out the fuel filter. I think that did it. So far it seems to be running better. But still code 44. <p>So as you can see I have invested some time and money in trying to figure out the problem. This usually brings on the service engine light: At idle bring the hot engine up to between 2500 & 3000 rpm and hold it there for around 45 seconds. The engine will hold steady and suddenly it drops about 500 rpm and the engine light comes on. It won't go off if I hold the engine at 2500 rpm but if it is let idle again the light goes out. When I check the code it comes up code 44. <p>Don't mean to go on here but I want to try and give an accurate account of what is going on. Any assitance or new ideas would be much appreciated. <p><br>Next request for info is this; Is there any sense to adding a turbo / blower whatever to this 2.5L engine even if they do make one. Or would the same ammount of money / time be better invested in an engine upgrade. I wanted this vehichle partly as an economy, fun ride but also want a good pass off point toward performance with reliability. (I know...buy a japanese car! ugh)<p><br>Ozzy. <p>P.S. Anybody looking for drop in front speaker replacements, I just bought some Kenwood 2 ways from The Good Guys for $90.00. They are 4 ohm, 160 watt peak rated. They were easy to install with out modifications and they sound pretty good. <p><p><p>
Z||000001||Cooter||08-07-99||05:31 PM||||Unplug the tach filter and see if the problem goes away. Mine was doing the same as yours and a new filter cured it.<p>As for performance out of the Iron Duck, just enjoy driving the little beast for what it is. If someone has already built it to over 100 horsepower, you are doing good. These engines will attain a certain amount of power, but some question their durability. Your drivability problems may be caused by whatever internal modifications (cam etc. . .) have been done to the engine. I am sure others here at the forum will have more insight to your problems.
Z||000002||Ozzy||08-07-99||09:45 PM||||Thnx Cooter...<br>I'll try the tach filter next.<p>Ozzy
Z||000003||lowCG||08-08-99||12:11 AM||||Sounds like you've got a decent setup,the 2.5 is only 300 cc less than the V6,that weighs more,has more moving parts,is harder to work on.<br>If you want to play with the fuel pressure,to try and accomodate the modifications done to your motor,the regulator is adjustable,and along with the idle air valve,can iron out just about anything.I added one turn to the regulator on a basically stock engine,exhaust changes only;wouldn't run without hesitating until I did this.<br>Fuel pressure will be best where,at wide open throttle,the car performs best,maybe a little bit on the lean side,as there is a range,and efficiency is lost by being on the rich side.After setting this,you can fine tune the driveability by adjusting the idle air control valve.<br>Good luck.
Z||000004||Cooter||08-08-99||03:48 PM||||How do you adjust the fuel pressure and idle air valve????
Z||000005||Ozzy||08-08-99||10:06 PM||||Hey Cooter..<p>I found how to ajust the pressure regulator but the Idle air valve has me stumped.<p>I don't claim to be the wiz at this at all but God gave me at least half a brain and I hope it works most of the time!(lol)<p>Anyway, I found the pressure regulator on the throttle body, which incedently is a different setup than the Hanes manual shows, but none the less I removed the 4 Torx screws that hold the regulator to the throttle body (which the manual also tells you not to do) and carefully removed and inspected it. I also removed the injector to inspect the filters and figure out how it all works. After checking out the regulator and dissasembling it (hoping I was not about to screw something up) I found a threaded screw under the spring base plate. BUT...<p>The outer end of the screw is sealed from the factory. Now I like to live dangerously so I drilled out the metal VERY carefully until I got to the screw. I first took care to count the threads exposed on the screw to create a reference for myself. As I drilled away the metal I reached the screw metal but this seal was made with the intention of not being messed with it seems as there is no screw head revealed but the screw began to turn with the drill. Fortunatley I had measured how far the screw was out so I moved it in 1 turn (toward the Throttle body).<p>Again, I'm no expert but it appears to me that the extra turn in will create more pressure from the spring onto the seal plate that impedes the flow of fuel back into the return fuel system, thus creating more fuel pressure in the injector fuel chamber.<p>What chages did it make??? Well, I think it cleaned up the hesitation some. It seems to rev out under load a little smoother but it's hard to say. I did not have time to do any comparisons so more latter. As for the IAC adjustment I could find absolutly nothing and I looked it over pretty good...<p>lowCG...help!!!!<p>Ozzy
Z||000006||lowCG||08-08-99||11:09 PM||||My car was an '86,pretty sure the rest are similar,anyways,I rotated,by hand(fingers)the cone shaped portion that sticks out of the IAC valve,while holding the spring back.<br>Sounds like you took care not to contaminate anything inside your injection system,a practice I take for granted in knowing,but failed to mention it in my post.<br>I found that one change,or an adjustment of one component wasn't enough to totally straighten out the running,it took all to do it.If you go too rich on the regulator,you exceed the parameters of the ECM,and what the oxygen sensor can correct,then creating a condition where you don't lean out at higher revs,negating any gains on the bottom, so a little is all you get without going in there,and re-programming.Sounds like your engine may be modified to the point of needing this possibly.<br>Unless you have access to easy programming changes,you might try a switch on the throttle(hand held to first test)that disengages the resistance in the coolant temp circuit at larger throttle openings.Just an idea. <br>
Z||000007||Ozzy||08-08-99||11:35 PM||||Hey lowCG...<p>I tryed pushing in the plunger of the IAC and it didn't budge. Also the plunger rod appeared keyed. <p>What type of hardware/software and equip. is required to do these programming changes you mentioned?? <p>I don't ever expect the "iron duck" as Cooter called it, to stand on it's back wheels but I would like to get it running correctly and producing all it can for maybe another $1,000.00 max at this point. The engine has a new rebuild and untill it requires removal and teardown for some reason, at which time I would consider an engine upgrade, I want to get my moneys worth out of it. <p>I'm kinda enjoying working on it myself and will tackle most things armed with the right info. This saves me some bucks so I can put the cash towards other upgrades. Also I need to put all new bushings and shocks on soon. It feels a little slushy on the highway so this will take away from the budget also.<p>Ozzy
Z||000008||ChadMan||08-09-99||07:32 AM||||When I bought my 88 Coupe it did the same thing as yours, Ozzy.  It would start sputtering and missing at 4000 rpm under full throttle.  Often the idle would drop to about 400 like it was going to stall, then it would rev up to about 1200 and settle back down to 900 and the check engine light would come on.  Also under steady mid-throttle acceleration up hills it would start to stumble and the light came on.  Always the same problem - code 44, lean exhaust.<p>The original owner first reported the problem to the dealer when the car had 2200 (!) miles on it.  They kept replacing things until he hit 12K (no more warranty), then told him the head was cracked, which sounds like bull$hit to me.  He called GM and got no help at all.<p>When I got the car, I spent about $600 and a lot of time replacing nearly all sensors, checked all wiring from the ECM back to each component, rebuilt the entire TBI, replaced the intake manifold gasket, and tried a new ECM from Ed Parks (he took it back when it didn't fix the problem - he's great).<p>Eventually I gave up and just drove it like that.  Believe it or not, the problem slowly started going away by itself!  Today the car pulls strong (well, for a Duke) to 5000 and only occasionally does its "idle hiccup" thing.  The light only comes on frigid winter mornings.<p><br>I hope I didn't bore you with this long story - I just thought misery might love company.  But if/when you find out what's causing your problem, I'd love to hear about it.
Z||000009||lowCG||08-09-99||11:42 AM||||Well it's been over five years since I've adjusted anything on a Fiero,but I know that little thing is adjustable,if not by any other way,adding or subtracting the gasket thickness will yield a change.<br>I believe there was more than one type,maybe try to get a spare of a different type(mine moved).<br>I also recall the changes in personality,like when the battery was disconnected,it would run funny for awhile,then straighten out very gradually.
Z||000010||Ozzy||08-10-99||04:11 PM||||Hey lowCG...<p>I see what your saying about the IAC. IF I add a gasket to the base of the IAC, assuming the adjustment you originally suggested won't work as I am yet to try that, how thick approx.??<p>Hey now that you mention the dissconected battery thing, I have disconnected the battery a couple of times to clear codes. I wonder...<br>I also have a '97 F250 with the new 5.4L Triton. They tell you in the manual that if the battery has been disconnected for any reason, that when reconnected you must allow the engine to idle at least one minute so the chip can re-learn it's "idle conditions".<br>Is there any learning curve to the ECM chip??<p>Ozzy
Z||000011||lowCG||08-10-99||07:12 PM||||Just how thick of a gasket depends on the requirement of your motor,you'll need to find an assortment,but I'd find an adjustable valve in a junkyard first.<br>Some control modules(VW)continue to learn based on your driving style,don't know about the GM ones,but I'm sure they do to some degree,after a reboot.
Z||000012||Ozzy||08-10-99||10:24 PM||||Thnx for all the info lowCG...I tryed placing a temp very thin gasket under the IAC plate. I believe I found a bit of a boost in the lower end (1500 - 3000rpm) but I think it lost a little under load in higher gears. Not really conclusive testing though.<p>Hey Chadman...appreciate your story. I only care if my engine is running right. So far I have put only $80.00 into sensors and I'm not sure but if you have read the prior posts in the thread...I think the pressure reg. adjustment has cleaned up the problem.<p>Ozzy
Z||000013||theogre||08-11-99||07:37 AM||||the 87+ 4cyl TBI is a 700 series unit, and yes its comletely diferant from the older unit. The fuel pressure is not adjustable. The IAC motor is also not adjustable. Any changes you make to the IAC will be negated by the ECM next time it recalibrates.<p>One thing you appear to have missed is checking out the DIS coils. If one/both are weak the motor will break up at high RPM. There is no tach filter on a DIS motor as far as I can find. Accel now makes an aftermarket DIS coil for these motors. if you replace the coils, they should be done as a set. even if you only use stock grade ones. The coil controller could also be causing you trouble. It contains the crank sensor.
Z||000014||lowCG||08-11-99||09:41 AM||||...and a weak spark prefers a rich mixture,so that may explain things,possibly.<br>Is there a resistance check for the coils on those later motors?
Z||000015||theogre||08-11-99||01:21 PM||||not really any way to test them yourself. resistance of the winding could be fine, but if the insulation has even the smallest crack you can lose energy. If they look like they are old especially if they show any damage at all, I'd just replace them and be done with it. They aren't cheap, but they aren't worth fooling with. If some nit dropped one while it was off, that's all it takes to ruin it.<p>make sure all the connector to the DIS controller on the block are good & tight. if one of them is messed up you'll have trouble too. The controller reports crank data to the ECM, as well as actually fires the coils. You might want to pull the controller off the block and make sure it not been beat on durring the rebuild.
Z||000016||Ozzy||08-11-99||09:57 PM||||Hey Humans... I feel priveledged; The great ogre responds! :-) I've read a lot of your posts in many different threads and you seem very up on stuff. Your input here is much appreciated as are the suggestions from everybody else who offered a contribution.<p>Hey this info is all awesome!!! I'm slowly plotting the death of the authors/editor of the Haynes manual. Anywun wanna help?? It even has an addendum for '87 & '88 Fiero's. Why the @#$%^%%$#$ @#%$#  doesn't it describe my throttle body?! <p>Now that you mention the recalibration thing, I noticed when I added the gasket to the IAC the engine would not idle at first and then it seamed to learn the new contitions and began to function at 'normal' idle. What about the fuel pressure reg. adjustment? It seemed to make sense to me.<br>The dude rebuilt the engine told me that he had replaced the DIS or coilpacks as he called them but some of his other mods are a bit rough, wiring in particular so maybe he could have damaged them accidently. He seemed to luv that car but...dunno!<p>Is there a decent shop manual that actually describes the stuff on MY year and model??<p>Is this throttle body better...is there a better after market job available worth the effort? Is porting and polishing something I can do myself and also worth the effort?<p>Again Thnx people. I'm an information soak 'cause I've caught the bug. Hope to able to assist others as I learn.<p>Ozzy<p>
Z||000017||Ozzy||08-11-99||10:02 PM||||Damn...just read my own post. Hope to be able to lern 2 spelll and do centenses son too. brb<p>Ossy
Z||000018||theogre||08-11-99||10:42 PM||||a proper, and opinions on whats proper vary, port & polish is a good thing. I Don't recomend anyone try this at home without allot of practice, and some training by someone who's done allot of it. Porting isn't just a mater or port matching. you need to know what type finish works where, and half a dozen other things at least.<p>the Helms manuals are year specific. They are the same manuals GM gives it's dealers pretty much. There is also a book, Petersons I think, that covers the 700 TBI as well as a bunch of others in detail.<p>The 700 is a better design in some ways. For one it's a simpler, cleaner, layout in many ways.<p>The IAC is recalibrated each time the car runs. The ECM closes the IAC comletely after the car hits around 35mph. This number then becomes the "home" value. All Idle calcs are based on that number. I recomend that you remove any modification you've made to the IAC. You must "screw in" the IAC pintle before you reinstall it. the manual you have is corect in the procedure for that. If you don't do it, you'll wreck the IAC, and maybe the TBI body.<p>The 700 TBI does Not allow fuel reg adjustment. Don't screw with it. Don't try to stretch the spring to get higher pressure. If you altered the FP reg, then you didn't do youself any big favors. GM really ment for you not to mess with it, don't mess with it more. If you can find another 700 grab it. You may need the parts.<p>I've dealt with the Haynes people thru e-mail. If anyone wants to go postal on them its fine with me. They are basically in this business for the money. they really don't give a shit if the books are acurate, complete, or updated. They are fully aware they are the only game in town.<br>
Z||000019||lowCG||08-12-99||01:30 AM||||I braved the mildewed copy of a Chilton's manual I found wadded up in the trunk of my recently purchased car.Normally I have Chilton's burning rituals upon finding them,so,doing the pre-ritual thumbing of the pages I was very surprised to find that there was a good amount of info inside,details of the later injection is included.<br>The fuel pressure is definately going to affect your curve,good or bad depending on what the modified motor will require.<br>GM didn't build this one the way it is now.<p>
Z||000020||GREENDANO||08-12-99||08:02 PM||||All right, my $0.02 worth.  Everything's adjustable.  Stock V6 FP regulators aren't adjustable either, but I adjusted mine for my motor.  When you tightened the screw on the regulator cover you raised the fuel pressure.  Hence the increase in performance.  From the sounds of what the previous owner told you about mods done to the motor, there is no way  the stock calibrations will supply enough fuel to keep up.  There is only a certain window of the pre-set fuel map that the ECM can control.  If you alter mechanical pieces of a motor and upset air flow/fuel needs, the stock ECM can't keep up on it's own without outside help.  <br>  Dan-O
Z||000021||Ozzy||08-13-99||10:36 PM||||Hey GREENDANO... Damn! Almost like I'm about to step in a puddle of shit with the differences of opinion here. It's still way cool for me though. Always good to have contrast so long as I don't ruin an engine in the process. Hey ogre, I'm curious about the IAC pintle you mentioned. As I understand it now, the pintle is like...keyed and the cone shaped gate on the end of the pintle does not rotate at all...or am I misunderstanding what your saying? On the 700 TBI...Are they that hard to get and are the IAC and regulator individually obtainable? Does anyone know of a decent after market replacent with readily available replacement parts? Can he ask another million questions they all ask!!!<br>Also I noticed a very small amount of ware on one side of the IAC gate. This was before I messed with it. <p>Hey... Ritual burning is good! Postal is good! I'm gonna look fer a better manual.<br>I have only this opinion to add;<br>Haynes (Haines) is a great name for underwear but this Haynes will be on my ass for a completley DIFFERENT reason!<p>OZZY
Z||000022||theogre||08-14-99||09:31 AM||||the whole pintle pushes into the IAC. you rock it, as you push in to the IAC. be gentle with it.<p>The IAC is a seperate part, as is the fuel reg diaphram. If you need the diaphram, then just get the rebuild kit. It usually only a few dollars more then just the diaphram. The fuel reg dome, and spring are not available seperate.<p>The 700's are fairly rare around here. I've 0nly seen 2 used one in decent shape.
Z||000023||Ozzy||08-14-99||12:04 PM||||no aftermarket replacements huh?
Z||000024||theogre||08-14-99||05:48 PM||||BWD, a Borg/Warner company, makes most of what parts are available. Wells makes some of the others.<p>There is no aftermarket unit, like the Holley altered 600 that replace the 700.
Z||000025||lowCG||08-15-99||02:01 AM||||Yeah,Haynes manuals have taken over the Chilton's realm of semi-worthlessness,they used to tell all,detailed specs on everything it seemed.<br>Don't think you'll ruin any motor by making it more driveable,just depends on how radical the cam is,and whether the stock computer can keep up.Also suggest a 180DEG thermostat,Kendall 20/50 oil,for good measure.
Z||000026||Bill 88||08-16-99||01:36 PM||||I think that Haynes ( should be called bandits) ar eonly in this for the money.  I purchased a manual on the A/C and it was worse than useless.  There was no new info, the only thing that proved that the book should be thrown-away was the statement in the book "the newer systems are using <br>R-134a. At this point we do not have any information on its use."  Now I purchased this manual two  months ago.  The outside did not mention the book was 15 years old.  Nor were they willing to return the purchase price.  The store I bought it from would not even consider returning my money.  They said they had too many problems with manuals from Haynes with incorrect info or badly written procedures.  They were trying to find a better company than Haynes But were just selling these books to get rid of them.  
